\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces The observation chain (top) and the exploratory simulators (bottom, in orange). The simulators share NASADEM terrain elevation but neither feed into nor validate radar measurements.}}{18}{figure.caption.4}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Acquisition geometry in a cross-track plane perpendicular to the orbit. Slant range $r$ connects the satellite to the target; $\theta _e$ is the elevation angle at the sensor and $\theta _i$ is the incidence angle at the target. Drawn over a flat Earth for clarity; Section~\ref {sec:incidencia} accounts for Earth curvature.}}{23}{figure.caption.8}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Effect of speckle and multilooking on a \emph {synthetic} test scene (computer-simulated, not a NISAR granule). From left to right: true underlying surface with five backscatter levels; 1-look realization; $2\times 2$ multilook average; and $4\times 4$ multilook average. All panels are displayed in dB using an identical grayscale stretch.}}{25}{figure.caption.10}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Speckle probability distributions for $L=1,4,16$. Left: in linear power scale, the exponential distribution ($L=1$) is strongly skewed. Right: in dB scale, distributions become nearly bell-shaped but retain a long left tail toward low dB values. Each curve is computed from 400,000 simulated samples.}}{26}{figure.caption.12}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Left: speckle standard deviation in dB as a function of $L$ (exact trigamma curve vs.\ $4.34/\sqrt {L}$ asymptote). Right: Jensen's inequality bias due solely to speckle, starting at 2.51\,dB for $L=1$ and decaying toward zero as $L$ increases.}}{27}{figure.caption.13}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Ellipsoidal incidence vs.\ local topographic incidence. On a radar-facing slope, the local incidence angle is smaller than the ellipsoidal angle (yielding stronger return and, in extreme cases, \emph {layover}); on the opposite slope it is larger and, if it reaches or exceeds $90^\circ $, produces radar shadow. The Stage 1 GCOV product does not apply an automated shadow/layover mask (\texttt {shadowMaskingApplied = False}).}}{28}{figure.caption.14}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces Left: $10\log _{10}$ compresses six orders of magnitude in linear power into a 60\,dB interval. Right: the inverse mapping from decibels to linear power ratio (highlighting $+3$\,dB $\to 2\times $ and $+10$\,dB $\to 10\times $).}}{32}{figure.caption.20}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces Change detection workflow in TerraPulse AI: Multilooking is applied in linear power to each comparable epoch prior to dB differencing and robust MAD standardization. Physical interpretation remains strictly separate from radiometric measurement.}}{34}{figure.caption.23}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces Visualization of the $\Delta \ensuremath {\gamma ^{0}}_{\mathrm {dB}}$ change map in the TerraPulse AI web interface (\textbf {Change $\Delta $} mode) over an active ROI in Chiapas. The diverging blue--white--red palette ($-6\,\mathrm {dB}$ to $+6\,\mathrm {dB}$) maps spatial areas where backscatter decreased (blue), remained stable (white), or increased (red) between $t_1$ and $t_2$.}}{35}{figure.caption.24}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10}{\ignorespaces Theoretical probability distribution of $\Delta \sigma ^0_{\mathrm {dB}}$ when \emph {no} true ground change has occurred---only independent speckle at each date---for $L=1,4,16$ equivalent looks (400,000 simulated pairs).}}{35}{figure.caption.25}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {11}{\ignorespaces Sidebar change-map controls and telemetry panel (\nolinkurl {ChangeControls.tsx}) alongside ROI statistics. Displays areas decreasing ($\downarrow $) or increasing ($\uparrow $) beyond $\tau _{\mathrm {dB}}$, median shift $m_{\Delta }$, interactive Multilook resolution ($20/50/100\,\mathrm {m}$) and threshold ($\pm 1.5$ to $\pm 6\,\mathrm {dB}$) selectors, empirical noise floor $\sigma _{\mathrm {MAD}}$, ratio $\tau _{\mathrm {dB}}/\sigma _{\mathrm {MAD}}$, percentage of pixels with $|z_{\mathrm {rob}}|>3$, and the automated warning when $\tau _{\mathrm {dB}} < 3\,\sigma _{\mathrm {MAD}}$.}}{38}{figure.caption.27}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12}{\ignorespaces The three fundamental backscatter mechanisms. In each panel, the blue arrow represents the incident microwave pulse and the red arrows represent energy scattered back toward the radar.}}{39}{figure.caption.28}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {13}{\ignorespaces Critical Rayleigh roughness height in L-band as a function of incidence angle $\theta _i$. For $\lambda =24$\,cm at $\theta _i=44^\circ $, the threshold is $4.2$\,cm: surfaces smoother than $4.2$\,cm act specularly; rougher surfaces scatter diffusely.}}{40}{figure.caption.31}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {14}{\ignorespaces Canopy volume and attenuated soil contributions to total $\sigma ^0$ in the water cloud model. Parameters $A$, $B$, and $\sigma ^0_{\text {soil}}$ are illustrative values chosen to show curve behavior rather than field-calibrated constants.}}{41}{figure.caption.33}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {15}{\ignorespaces Mapping between linear $\gamma ^0_{HH}$ value $v$ and grayscale tone $t$ in Figure~A. Colorbar ticks are evenly spaced in $t$, not in $v$.}}{44}{figure.caption.42}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {16}{\ignorespaces Spatial scale relationships across Stage 1 figures. The native GCOV grid has 10\,m pixels; Figures~B and B2 apply $2\times 2$ linear block averaging (20\,m pixels); Figure~A decimates 1 out of every 50 pixels without averaging (500\,m stride).}}{44}{figure.caption.43}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {17}{\ignorespaces Illustrative linear-power values across a $4\times 4$ pixel array. The four $2\times 2$ blocks contain 4, 2, 4, and 1 valid pixels, respectively.}}{45}{figure.caption.45}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {18}{\ignorespaces Gaussian dB reference benchmark. A Gaussian curve with the observed HH mean and standard deviation ($-8.055$ and $3.59$\,dB) would place the dB of the linear mean at $-6.57$\,dB, far to the left of the actual observed value ($-3.786$\,dB).}}{47}{figure.caption.49}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {19}{\ignorespaces Schematic of the rectangular bounding window vs.\ the ROI polygon interior. Inside the polygon lie valid pixels plus a narrow strip with \texttt {mask}~$=0$; outside the polygon lie margin pixels excluded from analysis.}}{48}{figure.caption.51}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {20}{\ignorespaces Geocentric triangle formed by the Earth's center $O$, the satellite $S$, and the ground target $T$ (schematic, not to scale).}}{52}{figure.caption.60}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {21}{\ignorespaces Left: incidence angle $\theta _i$ varies by $\approx 3^\circ $ across the ROI, from $\approx 45.4^\circ $ in the West (far range) to $\approx 42.3^\circ $ in the East (near range), with linear slope $-0.049^\circ $/km. Right: the corresponding flat-Earth factor $10\log _{10}\cos \theta _i$ varies by only $\approx 0.2$\,dB ($-1.31$ to $-1.54$\,dB).}}{52}{figure.caption.62}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {22}{\ignorespaces The pixel processing pipeline: from raw HDF5 reading to decibel scale. Execution order is critical: spatial averaging is performed \emph {before} converting to decibels.}}{54}{figure.caption.64}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {23}{\ignorespaces Mass balance inside a computational cell of width $\Delta x$. Water depth $h$ is measured vertically from bed elevation $z_b$ to free-surface elevation $\eta =z_b+h$. Unit discharge $q_x$ enters across the left face and exits across the right face, while rainfall rate $r$ adds depth from above.}}{59}{figure.caption.69}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {24}{\ignorespaces Modal DG2 representation inside a 1D cell. The true continuous profile (gray) is approximated by a piecewise linear function (blue); $W_0$ is the cell mean and $W_{1x}$ controls the slope. Face values are $W_0\pm \sqrt {3}\,W_{1x}$ (circles) and internal Gauss quadrature points are $W_0\pm W_{1x}$ (squares).}}{60}{figure.caption.71}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {25}{\ignorespaces Structure of the HLL approximate Riemann fan at a cell interface. Two bounding wave speeds ($S^-$ and $S^+$) separate the left state $U_L$, an intermediate state $U^*$, and the right state $U_R$.}}{61}{figure.caption.73}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {26}{\ignorespaces Hydrostatic reconstruction at a wet--dry interface. The left cell is wet while the right cell is dry with a higher bed elevation. The effective right-hand depth evaluates to $0$, preventing unphysical uphill flux.}}{61}{figure.caption.75}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {27}{\ignorespaces CFL stability time-step limit for 20\,m grid cells with $C_{\mathrm {CFL}}=0.33$. In a 1\,cm film, $\Delta t_{\max }\approx 21$\,s; at $h=1$\,m still water, $2.1$\,s; at $h=0.1$\,m with flow speed $0.5$\,m/s, $4.4$\,s.}}{61}{figure.caption.77}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {28}{\ignorespaces Left: dimensionless friction factor $C_f=gn_M^2/h^{1/3}$ vs.\ water depth $h$ for three Manning $n_M$ values. Right: friction slope $S_f$ at flow speed $u=1$\,m/s. At identical $n_M$, bed resistance increases sharply in thin water films.}}{62}{figure.caption.78}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {29}{\ignorespaces Computational grid layout. The user ROI (green) and enclosing hydraulic domain (dashed blue) are reprojected to a metric UTM CRS; the aligned computational rectangle (black) adds a 2-cell margin on all sides. Rainfall is injected exclusively at cell centers (green dots) lying inside the ROI polygon.}}{63}{figure.caption.79}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {30}{\ignorespaces Illustration of the \texttt {uniform\_balanced} temporal rainfall profile (10 intervals oscillating around a 20\,mm/h mean): symmetric $\pm d_j$ pairs cancel exactly so the integrated rainfall depth matches the prescribed 20\,mm total.}}{63}{figure.caption.80}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {31}{\ignorespaces Conceptual stages of the solar irradiance pipeline. The final projection combines the solar unit vector, clear-sky DNI, and the DEM surface normal.}}{81}{figure.caption.90}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {32}{\ignorespaces Clear-sky DNI structure (as a fraction of extraterrestrial $I_0$ at sea level) vs.\ solar zenith angle $\theta _z$ for four Linke turbidity values $T_L$.}}{82}{figure.caption.91}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {33}{\ignorespaces The incidence angle $\theta $ between facet normal $\hat {\mathbf {n}}$ and solar vector $\hat {\mathbf {s}}$ determines the projected DNI fraction $\cos \theta $. Shown: slope $\beta =25^\circ $, solar zenith $\theta _z=40^\circ $, and a slope facing away from the Sun ($\theta =65^\circ $).}}{83}{figure.caption.92}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {34}{\ignorespaces Projected DNI fraction $\max (0,\cos \theta )$ vs.\ terrain slope $\beta $ at solar zenith $\theta _z=50^\circ $ for three slope orientations. A sun-facing slope gains irradiance until its normal aligns with the sunbeam ($\beta =50^\circ $); a back-facing slope loses irradiance until reaching self-shadowing cutoff at $\beta =40^\circ $.}}{83}{figure.caption.93}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {35}{\ignorespaces Synthetic two-hill topography (left) and geometric projection ratio $\max (0,\hat {\mathbf {n}}\!\cdot \!\hat {\mathbf {s}})/\cos \theta _z$ relative to flat terrain (right), with the Sun at azimuth $135^\circ $ (SE) and $40^\circ $ elevation (20\,m grid).}}{84}{figure.caption.94}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {36}{\ignorespaces Diurnal solar elevation curves across three representative dates at latitude $16.8^\circ $\,N. The summer curve passes near the zenith; the winter curve peaks lower in the southern sky over a shorter day.}}{85}{figure.caption.96}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {37}{\ignorespaces Client-side processing pipeline of the 3D Terrain Viewer (\nolinkurl {web/src/utils/terrain3d.ts}). RGB-to-meters elevation decoding occurs at native $1:1$ tile resolution prior to any spatial interpolation.}}{87}{figure.caption.98}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {38}{\ignorespaces Interactive 3D topographic block model in \texttt {Terrain3DViewer} with \emph {Satellite (Esri World Imagery)} texture, $2.0\times $ vertical exaggeration, thin dark-slate pedestal, and right-hand controls and telemetry sidebar ($\Delta z$, $h_{\min }$, $h_{\max }$, extent, and tile zoom).}}{90}{figure.caption.99}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {39}{\ignorespaces The same ROI rendered in \emph {Hypsometric (Elevation)} mode inside the 3D viewer. Coastal blue delineates $0\,\mathrm {m}$ water bodies, while the elevation color ramp and Lambertian hillshading~\eqref {eq:lambert-hillshade} highlight watershed divides and canyon walls.}}{91}{figure.caption.100}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {40}{\ignorespaces \emph {SAR Mask (Geometry)} mode draped over the 3D terrain block for the \textbf {Ascending (T62/F10)} pass. Green delineates reliable valleys and plains ($\alpha \le 15^\circ $), amber marks critical slopes ($>15^\circ $), and red/indigo highlight canyon walls affected by \emph {layover} and radar shadow.}}{94}{figure.caption.102}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {41}{\ignorespaces Geometric inversion upon toggling to the \textbf {Descending (T55/F80)} pass in the 3D viewer sidebar. East/West-facing canyon walls swap their \emph {layover} (red) and radar shadow (deep indigo) regimes, updating class percentages in real time.}}{95}{figure.caption.103}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {42}{\ignorespaces Geometric illustration of Jensen's inequality for $10\log _{10}$: the secant chord connecting two points on the logarithmic curve lies strictly below the curve, so the average of the dB values (square) is smaller than the dB of the linear average (circle).}}{97}{figure.caption.104}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {43}{\ignorespaces Two rays reflected from surface elements differing in height by $h$. The two-way path difference is $2h\cos \theta _i$; the Rayleigh smoothness criterion requires this difference to remain below $\lambda /4$.}}{98}{figure.caption.105}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {44}{\ignorespaces Ritter's analytical dam-break profiles for $h_0=1$\,m at three instants. The wet--dry tip advances at $2\sqrt {gh_0}=6.26$\,m/s while the rarefaction wave retreats at $-\sqrt {gh_0}=-3.13$\,m/s.}}{100}{figure.caption.106}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {45}{\ignorespaces Client-side processing pipeline of the 3D Terrain Viewer (\nolinkurl {web/src/utils/terrain3d.ts}). RGB-to-meters elevation decoding occurs at native $1:1$ tile resolution prior to any spatial interpolation.}}{121}{figure.caption.198}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {46}{\ignorespaces Interactive 3D topographic block model in \texttt {Terrain3DViewer} with \emph {Satellite (Esri World Imagery)} texture, $2.0\times $ vertical exaggeration, thin dark-slate pedestal, and right-hand controls and telemetry sidebar ($\Delta z$, $h_{\min }$, $h_{\max }$, extent, and tile zoom).}}{124}{figure.caption.199}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {47}{\ignorespaces The same ROI rendered in \emph {Hypsometric (Elevation)} mode inside the 3D viewer. Coastal blue delineates $0\,\mathrm {m}$ water bodies, while the elevation color ramp and Lambertian hillshading~\eqref {eq:lambert-hillshade} highlight watershed divides and canyon walls.}}{125}{figure.caption.200}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {48}{\ignorespaces \emph {SAR Mask (Geometry)} mode draped over the 3D terrain block for the \textbf {Ascending (T62/F10)} pass. Green delineates reliable valleys and plains ($\alpha \le 15^\circ $), amber marks critical slopes ($>15^\circ $), and red/indigo highlight canyon walls affected by \emph {layover} and radar shadow.}}{128}{figure.caption.202}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {49}{\ignorespaces Geometric inversion upon toggling to the \textbf {Descending (T55/F80)} pass in the 3D viewer sidebar. East/West-facing canyon walls swap their \emph {layover} (red) and radar shadow (deep indigo) regimes, updating class percentages in real time.}}{129}{figure.caption.203}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {50}{\ignorespaces Client-side computational chain of the seismic simulator (\nolinkurl {web/src/utils/seismic.ts} and \nolinkurl {web/src/utils/signal.ts}).}}{130}{figure.caption.204}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {51}{\ignorespaces Seismic simulator panel and modal displaying the 2D attenuation curve $\mathrm {PGA}(D)$ versus epicentral distance, the logarithmic uncertainty band ($16\text {--}84\,\text {\char 37}$), scalar seismic moment $M_0$, and automatic validity-domain warnings for the selected GMPE model.}}{133}{figure.caption.209}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {52}{\ignorespaces 2D visual simulation on MapLibre GL showing the four geodesic isoseismal rings ($\mathrm {MMI}$), real-time concentric propagation of the $P$ (blue, $V_p=6.0\,\mathrm {km/s}$) and $S$ (red, $V_s=3.5\,\mathrm {km/s}$) wavefronts governed by Equation~\eqref {eq:radio-frente-superficie}, the interactive epicenter, and the receiving station selected on the map.}}{135}{figure.caption.210}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {53}{\ignorespaces Coupled view of the synthetic accelerogram time series $a(t)$ sampled at $50\,\mathrm {Hz}$ (top) and its Hann-windowed STFT time-frequency spectrogram on a $60\,\mathrm {dB}$ \emph {Inferno} scale (bottom). Both $P$-wave sidebands ($3.8$ and $5.2\,\mathrm {Hz}$ between $t_p$ and $t_s$) and the low-frequency energy concentration following $S$-wave arrival are clearly resolved alongside the $f_{\mathrm {dom}}$ and $D_{5\text {--}95}$ metrics.}}{137}{figure.caption.213}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {54}{\ignorespaces Four-layer decoupled architecture of TerraPulse AI. The scientific pipeline (\nolinkurl {pipeline/}) imports no web server modules; communication occurs through on-disk contracts and spatial tables in PostGIS.}}{139}{figure.caption.214}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {55}{\ignorespaces Comprehensive software architecture diagram of TerraPulse AI, illustrating the data flow from NASA Earthdata repositories (ASF Vertex and LP DAAC) to the Python backend (Django/DRF), the PostgreSQL 16 + PostGIS 3.4 Docker container, and the interactive React/Vite frontend modules.}}{139}{figure.caption.215}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {56}{\ignorespaces Inspection of the TerraPulse AI REST API JSON contracts (\texttt {/api/scenes}, \texttt {/api/roi-jobs}, and \texttt {/api/solar/simulations}), displaying HTTP headers, provenance fields (\texttt {code\_version}, \texttt {warnings}), and structured GeoJSON/metric payloads.}}{149}{figure.caption.222}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {57}{\ignorespaces PostgreSQL 16 + PostGIS 3.4 container running under Docker Compose along with a verification query over the spatial tables \texttt {scenes\_roi}, \texttt {scenes\_scene}, \texttt {scenes\_sceneinspection}, and \texttt {scenes\_scenechart}.}}{151}{figure.caption.224}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {58}{\ignorespaces Interactive \textbf {Solar \& Radiation Simulator} panel in the TerraPulse AI web interface, showing the incident direct irradiance heatmap ($G_i$ in $\mathrm {W/m^2}$) over NASADEM terrain with the \emph {Inferno} palette, the \textbf {Contrast ($p_2\text {--}p_{98}$)} / \textbf {Absolute ($0\text {--}\mathrm {DNI}$)} scale toggle, and the solar telemetry dashboard (SPA apparent elevation, clear-sky and adjusted DNI, median, and irradiance range across the ROI).}}{155}{figure.caption.225}%
